    "*   **What it means:** Out of all the *actual* positive samples (e.g., actual Spam or Positive Sentiment) in your test set, the model correctly identified **100% of them**.\n",
    "*   **The Finding:** There are **zero False Negatives**. The model did not miss a single positive instance. In scenarios like disease detection, this is the ultimate goal, but here it comes at a cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 2. Precision: 0.2000 (20%) — *The \"False Alarm\"*\n",
    "*   **What it means:** Out of all the samples the model *predicted* as positive, only **20% were actually positive**.\n",
    "*   **The Finding:** There is a high number of **False Positives**. For every 1 correct positive prediction, the model made 4 incorrect positive predictions. It is \"crying wolf\" too often.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 3. F1-Score: 0.3333 — *The \"Reality Check\"*\n",
    "*   **What it means:** The F1-Score is the harmonic mean of Precision and Recall. It is designed to penalize models that have a massive imbalance between the two metrics.\n",
    "*   **The Finding:** Because Precision is so low, it drags the overall F1-Score down to 0.33. This indicates that despite the perfect Recall, the model's overall reliability is quite poor.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 🔍 Diagnosis: Why did this specific combination happen?\n",
    "\n",
    "This exact pattern (Perfect Recall, Terrible Precision) is a classic mathematical artifact of **tiny test sets** or **model bias**.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Scenario A: The \"Small Dataset\" Effect (Most Likely)**\n",
    "In a small mock dataset (e.g., 30 sentences total), an 80/20 train-test split leaves you with a test set of only **6 sentences**.\n",
    "*   Imagine the 6-sentence test set contained only **1 actual Positive** sentence and **5 Negative** sentences.\n",
    "*   The Naive Bayes model predicted that **5 of the sentences were Positive**.\n",
    "*   **Recall Calculation:** It caught the 1 real Positive sentence. (1 True Positive / 1 Actual Positive = **1.00 or 100%**).\n",
    "*   **Precision Calculation:** It predicted 5 sentences as Positive, but only 1 was actually Positive. (1 True Positive / 5 Total Predicted Positives = **0.20 or 20%**).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Scenario B: Model Bias / Overfitting**\n",
    "The Naive Bayes model likely learned a very common word (like \"the\", \"a\", or a specific punctuation mark) that appears in almost every sentence. Because that word was heavily associated with the \"Positive\" class in the training data, the model just predicted \"Positive\" for almost everything in the test set."
   ]
